[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186129766 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our solid can be written as a type II region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 6yz+12\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{6yz+12}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[x]_{x=0}^{x=6yz+12}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(6yz+12)-0\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}6yz+12\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[3y{z}^{2}+12z]_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(3y(1)^{2}+12(1))-0\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3y+12\\,dy \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+12y]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}({2}^{2}-0)+12(2-0) \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281297 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our solid can be written as a type III region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,-1\\le z\\le 0,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 5-3{z}^{2}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{5-3{z}^{2}}dy\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}[y]_{y=0}^{y=5-3{z}^{2}}\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}5-3{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[5z-{z}^{3}]_{z=-1}^{z=0}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}0-(5(-1)-(-1)^{3})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}5-1\\,dx \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}dx \\\\  & =4[x]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =4(1-0) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186235408 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine the projection of the intersection of our surfaces onto the $yz$-plane. So, let's find where they intersect.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =-1+2{z}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}+2{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection in the $yz$-plane is an ellipse.\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type II region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,-2+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}\\le x\\le -1+2{z}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the interior of the ellipse ${y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}=1.$ \n\nAs a result, the volume of $R$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-2+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}^{-1+2{z}^{2}}\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1+2{z}^{2})-(-2+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1-{y}^{2}-2{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the boundary of $D$ consists of \n\n- the lower part $z=-\\sqrt{\\frac{1-{y}^{2}}{2}},$ and\n\n- the upper part $z=\\sqrt{\\frac{1-{y}^{2}}{2}},$\n\nfor $-1\\le y\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}1-{y}^{2}-2{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{(1-{y}^{2})/2}}^{\\sqrt{(1-{y}^{2})/2}}\\,1-{y}^{2}-2{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}+f & ={(-\\sqrt{\\frac{1-{y}^{2}}{2}})}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{\\frac{1-{y}^{2}}{2}})}^{2}+(1-{y}^{2}-2{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1-{y}^{2}}{2}+(1-{y}^{2}-2{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =2-2{y}^{2}-2{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281316 :question/worked-solution "![](images/8e/8e5f3095cac4e068e54d9b3ac2bb55dc4789e7ca68d4524dce4d25a55abd5be6.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type III region\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-2x-4z\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the projection of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane.\n\nTo find the top boundary curve of $D$ in the form $z=z(x),$ we substitute $y=0$  into the equation $y=4-2x-4z:$\n\n$$\n0=4-2x-4z\\,⟹\\,z=1-\\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we have\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-\\frac{x}{2}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/76/76fcca4251a904a08ac2f6ba8181d92c2c2fa7e07e596ef6276f1d21b574817a.png)\n\nHence, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-\\frac{x}{2},\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-2x-4z\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-2x-4z}dy\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/2}[y]_{y=0}^{y=4-2x-4z}\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/2}(4-2x-4z)-0\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/2}4-2x-4z\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[(4-2x)z-2{z}^{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=1-x/2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}((4-2x)(1-\\frac{x}{2})-2{(1-\\frac{x}{2})}^{2})-0\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(1-\\frac{x}{2})((4-2x)-2(1-\\frac{x}{2}))\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}(2-x)(2-x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2-2x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={[2x-{x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(2(2)-{2}^{2}+\\frac{{2}^{3}}{6})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-3acbe0bd-f475-593d-b042-bf6068b13815" :activity/id #uuid "3acbe0bd-f475-593d-b042-bf6068b13815" :activity/title "Calculating Volumes of Solids Using Triple Integrals" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048997 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-5f678a58-ebc6-50b7-bfbb-3b3bd2f77536" "step-c60f261b-2160-5d1d-a644-128afd94f52c" "step-2c68a282-fb8d-5473-b214-328eb1dfb504" "step-a29fc4a4-b0fb-5b9b-a936-4edfa87ff06b" "step-b8e034ac-74ed-5ee7-86d4-526d9fcddec2"] :activity/first-step "step-5f678a58-ebc6-50b7-bfbb-3b3bd2f77536" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-5f678a58-ebc6-50b7-bfbb-3b3bd2f77536" :step/id #uuid "5f678a58-ebc6-50b7-bfbb-3b3bd2f77536" :step/content 17592186129762 :step/next "step-c60f261b-2160-5d1d-a644-128afd94f52c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "